    "body": "Closes #6009.\n\nInside a function, `x = cmath.no_such_func()` (with `cimport libc.math as cmath`) reported \"cimported module has no attribute 'no_such_func'\" and then crashed in `AnalyseExpressionsTransform` with `AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'type'`.\n\n`AttributeNode.infer_type` calls `analyse_as_cimported_attribute_node`. In the error branch, that returns the node itself with no `entry`, and `infer_type` then reads `node.entry.type`. Module-level uses don't go through local type inference, which is why `tests/errors/cimport_attributes.pyx` didn't catch it.\n\nThe fix:\n- **Inference:** `infer_type` now returns `error_type` for that case.\n- **Error reporting:** inference no longer reports the error itself. It can run several times per node, and the error was otherwise printed three times. `analyse_types` reports it once, as it does at module level.\n\nThe new error test `tests/errors/cimport_attribute_infer_GH6009.pyx` covers a call and a plain attribute assigned to locals. On master it fails with the compiler crash; with this change it passes, and so does the rest of `tests/errors`. The issue's two-file example (`cimport bbug`) now stops after its two expected errors.\n\nI didn't have a C compiler available, so I didn't run the compile/run tests locally. This change only affects the branch that reports this error.\n",
